          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01ECTzsBLtiT936ALABoxWYV",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/single-composition-point.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Single Composition Point\\ndescription: How centralized startup configuration ensures consistent scoping of credentials and file paths across the Grimoire system.\\ntimestamp: 2025-04-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - concept/Single-Composition-Point\\n  - pattern/Composition\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] explicitly implement the same pattern \u2014 centralized, one-time resolution at startup rather than distributed discovery throughout the codebase.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2025-10-14\\n---\\n\\n## The Pattern\\n\\nBoth credential scoping and runtime-path resolution follow a shared architectural principle: **resolve sensitive or critical configuration exactly once at startup, in a single place, before any downstream code runs.** This centralizes control and prevents fragmentation.\\n\\n## Credential Scoping Connection\\n\\n[[credential-scoping]] uses this principle to isolate secrets: the Hub\u0027s startup path reads the Anthropic API key from a git-ignored file once, then injects it only into the child process environment of the Ingest or Query agent. The Hub itself never holds the credential. This works because credentials are composed at one moment (startup) and scoped to their proper boundary (the agent subprocess) before any potentially unsafe code can run.\\n\\n## Runtime Paths Connection\\n\\n[[runtime-paths]] applies the same principle to file locations: every filesystem path the Hub will ever touch \u2014 wiki root, database, secrets file, agent instruction directories \u2014 is resolved exactly once at startup by a single path resolver. Configuration sources (CLI, environment, config file, code defaults) are consulted in a fixed order, all required paths are validated, and the downstream codebase never re-discovers or re-reads working directories independently.\\n\\n## The Shared Benefit\\n\\nBoth decisions prevent the same class of problem: **configuration drift and distributed rediscovery.** If credentials were read multiple times or paths were resolved in different parts of the codebase, you risk:\\n\\n- Inconsistency (different subsystems seeing different values)\\n- Leakage (a careless discovery path might bypass the intended scope)\\n- Fragility (adding a new credential or path means patching many places instead of one resolver)\\n\\nBy composing both at the Hub\u0027s startup in centralized resolvers, Grimoire ensures that the system\u0027s security and operational boundaries are set up once and inherited consistently by everything downstream.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
