      "assistant_text": "## Answer\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions are two applications of **the same architectural pattern**: centralized, one-time composition at startup rather than distributed rediscovery throughout the system.\n\n**Credential Scoping** ([[credential-scoping]]): The Hub reads the Anthropic API key from a secrets file once at startup and immediately injects it only into the child process environment of the Ingest or Query agent \u2014 never into the Hub\u0027s own environment. This scopes the secret to the process that actually needs it, before any unsafe code runs.\n\n**Runtime Paths** ([[runtime-paths]]): The Hub runs a single path resolver at startup that reads configuration from CLI, environment, config files, or code defaults (in that order), validates every required location exists, and populates the entire resolved path set. Downstream code never re-discovers paths independently; it consumes the already-resolved set instead.\n\n**The Connection**: Both prevent **configuration drift and distributed rediscovery**. If credentials were read multiple times or paths resolved in different places, you risk inconsistency, leakage, and fragility. By composing both at startup in centralized resolvers, the system\u0027s security and operational boundaries are set once and inherited consistently by everything downstream.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]], since the relationship between these two decisions is a genuine pattern that no single existing page articulates.",
